<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Many forms collect a single value through several controls: a telephone number split into area
code, central office code, and line number; a date entered as a day, a month picked from a list,
and a year; a card expiry date in two <{select}> elements. Each of these forms currently needs
script to:

* combine the pieces into the one string the server expects, usually by copying them into a hidden
    field;
* add the separators that belong between the pieces, such as the parentheses and hyphen of a
    telephone number;
* check that the value is complete, and not just that each piece is valid on its own; and
* show one error message for the whole value, instead of one for each piece.

The <{collector}> element lets authors declare the value instead, and leaves the combining and
validating to the user agent.

<div class=example id=example-intro-phone>
    A form asks for a North American telephone number in three parts:

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <form action="/register">
      <label for="phone">Phone number</label>
      <collector id="phone" name="phone" required
                 pattern="({areaCode}) {centralOfficeCode}-{lineNumber}">
        (<input type="tel" name="areaCode" minlength="3" maxlength="3"
                autocomplete="tel-area-code" aria-label="Area code">)
        <input type="tel" name="centralOfficeCode" minlength="3" maxlength="3"
               autocomplete="tel-local-prefix" aria-label="Central office code">
        -
        <input type="tel" name="lineNumber" minlength="4" maxlength="4"
               autocomplete="tel-local-suffix" aria-label="Line number">
      </collector>
      <button>Register</button>
    </form>
    </xmp>

    The user types <kbd>206</kbd>, <kbd>555</kbd>, and <kbd>0123</kbd>. Submitting the form sends
    one entry:

    <pre>phone=(206) 555-0123</pre>

    The three <{input}> elements aren't submitted on their own. As the user fills in the fields, the
    collector's {{HTMLCollectorElement/value}} and validity change like this:

    <table class=data>
        <thead>
            <tr><th><code>areaCode</code><th><code>centralOfficeCode</code><th><code>lineNumber</code><th>{{HTMLCollectorElement/value}}<th>Validity
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><td><td><td>(empty string)<td>{{ValidityState/valueMissing}}, because the collector is <{collector/required}>
            <tr><td>206<td><td><td><code>(206) -</code><td>{{ValidityState/patternMismatch}}, because the pattern is only partly filled in
            <tr><td>206<td>555<td>01<td><code>(206) 555-01</code><td>{{ValidityState/tooShort}}, rolled up from <code>lineNumber</code>
            <tr><td>206<td>555<td>0123<td><code>(206) 555-0123</code><td>Valid
    </table>

    If the user submits the form too early, the user agent shows one message for the phone number,
    such as "Please fill out all parts of this field", and moves focus to the first empty part.
</div>

User agents that don't support this specification treat <{collector}> as an unknown element. The
controls inside it still work, and each is submitted as its own entry:
<code>areaCode=206&amp;centralOfficeCode=555&amp;lineNumber=0123</code>. A server that accepts both
forms works with both kinds of user agent.


<h2 id=the-collector-element>The <code>collector</code> element</h2>

<h3 id=element-definition>Element definition</h3>

<dl class=element>
    <dt>Categories:
    <dd>[=Flow content=].
    <dd>[=Phrasing content=].
    <dd>[=Listed=], [=labelable=], and [=submittable=] [=form-associated element=].
    <dd>[=Palpable content=].

    <dt>Contexts in which this element can be used:
    <dd>Where [=phrasing content=] is expected.

    <dt>Content model:
    <dd>[=Phrasing content=], but there must be no <{collector}> element descendants.

    <dt>Content attributes:
    <dd>Global attributes
    <dd><{collector/disabled}> — Whether the collector and the controls inside it are disabled
    <dd><{collector/form}> — Associates the element with a <{form}> element
    <dd><{collector/name}> — Name of the element to use for form submission and in the
        {{HTMLFormElement/elements|form.elements}} API
    <dd><{collector/pattern}> — Template that combines the collected controls' values
    <dd><{collector/required}> — Whether the value is required for form submission

    <dt>DOM interface:
    <dd>{{HTMLCollectorElement}}
</dl>

The <dfn element>collector</dfn> element [=represents=] a single value made up of the values of the
controls inside it, which are its [=collected controls=].

The <dfn element-attr for=collector>name</dfn>, <dfn element-attr for=collector>form</dfn>, and
<dfn element-attr for=collector>disabled</dfn> content attributes work as they do for other
[=form-associated elements=]. A <{collector}> element is [=form control/disabled=] if its
<{collector/disabled}> attribute is specified, or if it is a descendant of a [=fieldset/disabled=]
<{fieldset}> element and is not a descendant of that <{fieldset}> element's first <{legend}> element
child, if any.

The <dfn element-attr for=collector>required</dfn> attribute is a [=boolean attribute=]. When
specified, the collector's value is required. See [[#constraint-validation]].

Note: The <{collector}> element has no special rendering. It has no default styles, so it is
<code>display: inline</code>, as it is in user agents that don't support it. It needs no changes to
the HTML parser.

<h3 id=collectable-elements>Collectable elements</h3>

A <dfn export>collectable element</dfn> is either:

* an <{input}> element whose <{input/type}> attribute is in a state whose [=value mode=] is
    [=value mode/value=]; or
* a <{select}> element that does not have a <{select/multiple}> attribute.

Note: The states whose [=value mode=] is [=value mode/value=] are Text, Search, Telephone, URL,
Email, Password, Date, Month, Week, Time, Local Date and Time, Number, Range, and Color. Checkboxes,
radio buttons, file uploads, and buttons are not collectable. <{select}> elements with a
<{select/multiple}> attribute are excluded because they can hold more than one value.

The <dfn export>collector owner</dfn> of an element |element| is the nearest ancestor of |element|
that is a <{collector}> element, or null if there is no such ancestor.

The <dfn>candidate controls</dfn> of a <{collector}> element |collector| are the
[=collectable elements=] whose [=collector owner=] is |collector| and whose [=form owner=] is
|collector|'s [=form owner=], in [=tree order=].

Note: Because ancestors are found in the element's own [=tree=], controls in a shadow tree are never
collected by a <{collector}> element outside it.

<h3 id=the-pattern-attribute>The <code>pattern</code> attribute</h3>

The <dfn element-attr for=collector>pattern</dfn> content attribute is a template that says how the
values of the collected controls combine into the collector's value. It is made up of literal text
and <dfn>placeholders</dfn>. A placeholder is an <dfn>identifier</dfn> between U+007B ({) and U+007D
(}), such as <code>{areaCode}</code>. It stands for the value of the control whose <{input/name}>
attribute is that identifier.

To include a literal U+007B ({) or U+007D (}) in the collector's value, authors write it twice:
<code>&#123;&#123;</code> or <code>&#125;&#125;</code>.

<div class=example id=example-escaping>
    With an <code>areaCode</code> control whose value is <code>206</code>:

    <table class=data>
        <thead>
            <tr><th><{collector/pattern}><th>Value
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>({areaCode})</code><td><code>(206)</code>
            <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;{areaCode}&#125;&#125;</code><td><code>{206}</code>
            <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;areaCode&#125;&#125;-{areaCode}</code><td><code>{areaCode}-206</code>
            <tr><td><code>{areaCode}{areaCode}</code><td><code>206206</code>
    </table>

    In <code>&#123;&#123;{areaCode}&#125;&#125;</code>, the first two braces are an escaped <code>{</code>, the next brace
    starts the placeholder, and the last two braces are an escaped <code>}</code>.
</div>

A <dfn>pattern token</dfn> is a [=struct=] with two [=struct/items=]: a <dfn for="pattern token">kind</dfn>,
which is either "<code>literal</code>" or "<code>placeholder</code>", and a
<dfn for="pattern token">text</dfn>, which is a [=string=].

<div algorithm="parse a collector pattern">
    To <dfn export>parse a collector pattern</dfn> given a [=string=] |input|:

    1. Let |tokens| be an empty [=list=].

    1. Let |literal| be the empty string.

    1. Let |position| be a [=string/position variable=] for |input|, initially pointing at the start
        of |input|.

    1. [=iteration/While=] |position| is not past the end of |input|:

        1. Let |c| be the [=code point=] at |position| within |input|.

        1. If |c| is U+007B ({) or U+007D (}), and the code point after |position| is also |c|,
            then append |c| to |literal|, advance |position| by 2, and [=iteration/continue=].

        1. If |c| is U+007B ({), then:

            1. Let |start| be |position|.

            1. Advance |position| by 1.

            1. Let |identifier| be the result of [=collecting a sequence of code points=] that are
                neither U+007B ({) nor U+007D (}) from |input|, given |position|.

            1. If |identifier| is not the empty string, and |position| is not past the end of
                |input|, and the code point at |position| is U+007D (}), then:

                1. If |literal| is not the empty string, then [=list/append=] a new
                    [=pattern token=] whose [=pattern token/kind=] is "<code>literal</code>" and
                    whose [=pattern token/text=] is |literal| to |tokens|, and set |literal| to the
                    empty string.

                1. [=list/Append=] a new [=pattern token=] whose [=pattern token/kind=] is
                    "<code>placeholder</code>" and whose [=pattern token/text=] is |identifier| to
                    |tokens|.

                1. Advance |position| by 1.

                1. [=iteration/Continue=].

            1. <dfn>Unmatched brace</dfn>: Set |position| to |start|.

        1. Append |c| to |literal|, and advance |position| by 1. If |c| is U+007B ({) or U+007D (}),
            this is also an [=unmatched brace=].

    1. If |literal| is not the empty string, then [=list/append=] a new [=pattern token=] whose
        [=pattern token/kind=] is "<code>literal</code>" and whose [=pattern token/text=] is
        |literal| to |tokens|.

    1. Return |tokens|.
</div>

If the <{collector/pattern}> attribute is specified, its value must be a
<dfn export>valid collector pattern</dfn>: a string that, when [=parse a collector pattern|parsed=],
never reaches an [=unmatched brace=], and in which every [=placeholder=]'s [=identifier=] is
[=string/identical to=] the value of the <{input/name}> attribute of one of the collector's
[=candidate controls=].

Note: User agents are lenient with invalid patterns. A brace that is neither doubled nor part of a
placeholder is kept as literal text. A placeholder whose identifier matches no control is replaced
with nothing, and doesn't stop the collector from being complete.

Note: Identifiers are compared exactly. <code>{ areaCode }</code> does not match a control named
<code>areaCode</code>.

<h3 id=the-collected-controls>Collected controls</h3>

<div algorithm>
    The <dfn export>collected controls</dfn> of a <{collector}> element |collector| are the [=list=]
    returned by these steps:

    1. Let |candidates| be |collector|'s [=candidate controls=].

    1. If |collector| does not have a <{collector/pattern}> attribute, then return |candidates|.

    1. Let |tokens| be the result of [=parse a collector pattern|parsing=] the value of
        |collector|'s <{collector/pattern}> attribute.

    1. Let |result| be an empty [=list=].

    1. [=list/For each=] |control| of |candidates|:

        1. If |control| does not have a <{input/name}> attribute, then [=iteration/continue=].

        1. Let |name| be the value of |control|'s <{input/name}> attribute.

        1. If |tokens| does not [=list/contain=] a [=pattern token=] whose [=pattern token/kind=] is
            "<code>placeholder</code>" and whose [=pattern token/text=] is |name|, then
            [=iteration/continue=].

        1. If |result| already [=list/contains=] an element whose <{input/name}> attribute's value
            is |name|, then [=iteration/continue=].

        1. [=list/Append=] |control| to |result|.

    1. Return |result|.
</div>

An element is a <dfn export>collected control</dfn> if it is one of the [=collected controls=] of its
[=collector owner=].

Note: A control inside a collector that the pattern doesn't mention, such as a "Save this number"
checkbox or a text field with a different name, is not a collected control. It behaves like any
other control.

The <dfn export>collected value</dfn> of a [=collected control=] |control| is:

* the empty string, if |control| is [=form control/disabled=];
* if |control| is a <{select}> element, the [=option/value=] of the first <{option}> element in
    |control|'s [=list of options=] whose [=selectedness=] is true and that is not
    [=option/disabled=], or the empty string if there is no such <{option}> element;
* otherwise, |control|'s [=form control/value=].

The <dfn>enabled collected controls</dfn> of a <{collector}> element are its [=collected controls=]
that are not [=form control/disabled=].

A <{collector}> element is <dfn for=collector>empty</dfn> if the [=collected value=] of every one of
its [=enabled collected controls=] is the empty string. It is <dfn for=collector>complete</dfn> if
the collected value of every one of its enabled collected controls is not the empty string.

Note: A collector with no enabled collected controls is both empty and complete.

<h3 id=the-value>The collector's value</h3>

<div algorithm>
    The <dfn export for=collector>value</dfn> of a <{collector}> element |collector| is the result of
    these steps:

    1. If |collector| is [=collector/empty=], then return the empty string.

    1. If |collector| does not have a <{collector/pattern}> attribute, then return the
        [=string/concatenation=] of the [=collected values=] of |collector|'s
        [=collected controls=], in order.

    1. Let |result| be the empty string.

    1. [=list/For each=] |token| of the result of [=parse a collector pattern|parsing=] the value of
        |collector|'s <{collector/pattern}> attribute:

        1. If |token|'s [=pattern token/kind=] is "<code>literal</code>", then append |token|'s
            [=pattern token/text=] to |result|.

        1. Otherwise, if |collector|'s [=collected controls=] [=list/contain=] an element whose
            <{input/name}> attribute's value is |token|'s [=pattern token/text=], then append that
            element's [=collected value=] to |result|.

    1. Return |result|.
</div>

Note: An empty collector's value is the empty string, not the pattern's literal text. An optional
telephone number that the user leaves blank therefore submits <code>phone=</code>, not
<code>phone=() -</code>.

Note: The collector fires no events of its own. The <code>input</code> and {{HTMLElement/change}}
events of its collected controls bubble through it, so authors can listen for them on the
collector and read its {{HTMLCollectorElement/value}}.

<h3 id=the-dom-interface>The DOM interface</h3>

<xmp class=idl>
[Exposed=Window]
interface HTMLCollectorElement : HTMLElement {
  [HTMLConstructor] constructor();

  [CEReactions, Reflect] attribute boolean disabled;
  readonly attribute HTMLFormElement? form;
  [CEReactions, Reflect] attribute DOMString name;
  [CEReactions, Reflect] attribute DOMString pattern;
  [CEReactions, Reflect] attribute boolean required;

  readonly attribute DOMString type;
  readonly attribute DOMString value;

  readonly attribute boolean willValidate;
  readonly attribute ValidityState validity;
  readonly attribute DOMString validationMessage;
  boolean checkValidity();
  boolean reportValidity();
  undefined setCustomValidity(DOMString error);

  readonly attribute NodeList labels;
};
</xmp>

The <dfn attribute for=HTMLCollectorElement>disabled</dfn>,
<dfn attribute for=HTMLCollectorElement>name</dfn>,
<dfn attribute for=HTMLCollectorElement>pattern</dfn>, and
<dfn attribute for=HTMLCollectorElement>required</dfn> IDL attributes must [=reflect=] the content
attributes of the same name.

The <dfn attribute for=HTMLCollectorElement>form</dfn> IDL attribute returns the element's
[=form owner=], as it does for other [=listed=] [=form-associated elements=].

The <dfn attribute for=HTMLCollectorElement>type</dfn> getter steps are to return
"<code>collector</code>".

The <dfn attribute for=HTMLCollectorElement>value</dfn> getter steps are to return [=this=]'s
[=collector/value=].

The <dfn attribute for=HTMLCollectorElement>willValidate</dfn>,
<dfn attribute for=HTMLCollectorElement>validity</dfn>,
<dfn attribute for=HTMLCollectorElement>validationMessage</dfn>,
<dfn method for=HTMLCollectorElement>checkValidity()</dfn>,
<dfn method for=HTMLCollectorElement>reportValidity()</dfn>, and
<dfn method for=HTMLCollectorElement>setCustomValidity(error)</dfn> members work as they do for
other [=listed=] form-associated elements, as defined by the constraint validation API [[!HTML]],
with the additions in [[#constraint-validation]].

The <dfn attribute for=HTMLCollectorElement>labels</dfn> IDL attribute returns the element's
<{label}> elements, as it does for other [=labelable=] elements.

<div class=example id=example-idl>
    <xmp highlight=js>
    const phone = document.getElementById("phone");

    phone.value;                          // "(206) 555-0123"
    phone.validity.patternMismatch;       // true while the pattern is only partly filled in
    new FormData(phone.form).get("phone") // "(206) 555-0123"

    // React to changes in any of the pieces.
    phone.addEventListener("input", () => preview.textContent = phone.value);

    // Feature detection.
    const supported = "HTMLCollectorElement" in window;
    </xmp>
</div>


<h2 id=constraint-validation>Constraint validation</h2>

<h3 id=collector-constraints>The collector's constraints</h3>

A <{collector}> element is [=barred from constraint validation=] if it is [=form control/disabled=]
or has a <{datalist}> element ancestor.

A <{collector}> element that has a <{collector/required}> attribute and is [=collector/empty=] is
[=suffering from being missing=].

A <{collector}> element that is neither [=collector/empty=] nor [=collector/complete=] is
[=suffering from a pattern mismatch=].

Note: In other words, a collector is invalid while the user has filled in some parts of it and not
others. An optional collector that is left entirely blank is valid.

A <{collector}> element whose [=custom validity error message=] is not the empty string is
[=suffering from a custom error=].

In addition, the collector rolls up the validity of its pieces: for each of a
<{collector}> element's [=enabled collected controls=] that is a
[=candidate for constraint validation=], the collector suffers from every validity state that
control suffers from. For example, if a collected control is too short, the collector is
suffering from being too short, and its {{ValidityState/tooShort}} flag is true.

Note: Each collected control keeps its own validity. Authors can put <{input/required}>,
<a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#attr-input-minlength"><code>minlength</code></a>,
and <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#attr-input-pattern"><code>pattern</code></a>
attributes on the pieces, and style them with <code>:invalid</code>. The collector's validity is the
rollup of all of them.

<h3 id=the-problem-control>The problem control</h3>

The <dfn export>problem control</dfn> of a <{collector}> element |collector| is the first element,
in [=tree order=], among |collector|'s [=enabled collected controls=] that is [=focusable=] and
either has a [=collected value=] that is the empty string or is a
[=candidate for constraint validation=] that does not [=satisfy its constraints=]. If there is no
such element, it is the first focusable element among |collector|'s enabled collected controls. If
there is no such element either, it is null.

<h3 id=validation-message>The validation message</h3>

The {{HTMLCollectorElement/validationMessage}} of a <{collector}> element |collector| that does not
[=satisfy its constraints=] should be:

1. |collector|'s [=custom validity error message=], if that is not the empty string;
1. otherwise, if |collector| is [=suffering from being missing=] or
    [=suffering from a pattern mismatch=] because of its own constraints, a message from the user
    agent saying that the whole value is needed, such as "Please fill out all parts of this field";
1. otherwise, the {{HTMLInputElement/validationMessage}} of the first of
    |collector|'s [=enabled collected controls=], in [=tree order=], that does not satisfy its
    constraints.

<h3 id=reporting>Reporting problems</h3>

When the user agent reports a problem with a <{collector}> element |collector| to the user, whether
from {{HTMLCollectorElement/reportValidity()}} or from [=interactively validate the constraints=],
it should:

* show |collector|'s {{HTMLCollectorElement/validationMessage}}, positioned near |collector| or its
    [=problem control=]; and
* if |collector|'s [=problem control=] is not null, run the [=focusing steps=] for the problem
    control, instead of trying to focus |collector|.

<h3 id=patch-static-validation>Changes to statically validating the constraints</h3>

Because the collector rolls up the validity of its pieces, a form reports the collector, not each
piece. The algorithm to [=statically validate the constraints=] of a <{form}> element [[!HTML]] is
changed as follows. Inserted text is <ins>marked like this</ins>.

<div algorithm="statically validate the constraints (patched)">
    1. Let |controls| be a list of all the [=submittable=] elements whose [=form owner=] is
        <var ignore>form</var>, in [=tree order=].

    1. Let <var ignore>invalid controls</var> be an initially empty list of elements.

    1. For each element |field| in |controls|, in [=tree order=]:

        1. If |field| is not a [=candidate for constraint validation=], then move on to the next
            element.

        1. <ins>If |field| is a [=collected control=], then move on to the next element.</ins>

        1. [Otherwise, if |field| [=satisfies its constraints=], move on to the next element. This
            step and the rest of the algorithm are unchanged.]
</div>

Note: The <code>invalid</code> event therefore fires at the collector, and not at its collected
controls, when a form is submitted. Calling {{HTMLInputElement/checkValidity()}} on a collected
control directly still fires <code>invalid</code> at that control.

<h3 id=user-validity>User validity</h3>

A <{collector}> element's [=user validity=] is true if at least one of its [=collected controls=] has
a user validity of true, and either none of its collected controls is [=focused=] or every one of
them has a user validity of true. Otherwise, it is false.

Note: This means <code>:user-invalid</code> matches the collector once the user has changed one of
its pieces and then left the collector, or once a form submission has been attempted. It doesn't
match while the user is still moving through the pieces for the first time. Resetting the form
resets the pieces, and so the collector.

<div class=example id=example-styling>
    The collector's message and styles cover the whole value:

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <style>
      collector:user-invalid { outline: 2px solid crimson; }
      .error { display: none; color: crimson; }
      collector:user-invalid + .error { display: block; }
    </style>

    <label for="dob">Date of birth</label>
    <collector id="dob" name="dob" pattern="{year}-{month}-{day}" required
               aria-describedby="dob-error">
      <input name="day" inputmode="numeric" minlength="2" maxlength="2" aria-label="Day">
      <select name="month" aria-label="Month">
        <option value="">Month</option>
        <option value="01">January</option>
        <!-- … -->
        <option value="12">December</option>
      </select>
      <input name="year" inputmode="numeric" minlength="4" maxlength="4" aria-label="Year">
    </collector>
    <p class="error" id="dob-error">Enter your full date of birth.</p>
    </xmp>

    The pieces appear in the order the user expects, and the value comes out in the order the server
    expects: <code>dob=1990-07-04</code>. The <{select}> element's empty first option counts as an
    empty piece, so the collector isn't complete until the user picks a month.
</div>


<h2 id=form-submission>Form submission</h2>

<h3 id=patch-construct-the-entry-list>Changes to constructing the entry list</h3>

The algorithm for [=constructing the entry list=] [[!HTML]] is changed as follows. Inserted text is
<ins>marked like this</ins>. Steps that don't change are summarized in brackets.

<div algorithm="construct the entry list (patched)">
    5. For each element |field| in <var ignore>controls</var>, in [=tree order=]:

        1. If any of the following are true, then continue:

            * [The existing conditions, such as |field| being [=form control/disabled=], are
                unchanged.]
            * <ins>|field| is a [=collected control=].</ins>

        1. [If |field| is an Image Button, then append its coordinates and continue.]

        1. [If |field| is a form-associated custom element, then perform its entry construction
            algorithm and continue.]

        1. If either the |field| element does not have a <{input/name}> attribute specified, or its
            <{input/name}> attribute's value is the empty string, then continue.

        1. Let |name| be the value of the |field| element's <{input/name}> attribute.

        1. <ins>If |field| is a <{collector}> element, then [=entry list/create an entry=] with
            |name| and |field|'s [=collector/value=], append it to <var ignore>entry list</var>, and
            continue.</ins>

        1. [The remaining steps are unchanged.]
</div>

Note: {{FormData}}'s constructor and the {{HTMLElement/formdata}} event use the same algorithm.
Scripts calling <code>new FormData(form)</code> therefore see the collector's value, and none of its
collected controls, exactly as the server does.

Note: A form with <{form/novalidate}>, or one submitted with {{HTMLFormElement/submit()}}, skips
validation. A collector that is only partly filled in then submits its partial value, such as
<code>(206) -</code>, so that the server can tell it apart from a value the user left blank.


<h2 id=changes-to-html>Changes to the HTML Standard</h2>

To integrate this specification into the HTML Standard [[!HTML]], make these changes:

1. Add [[#the-collector-element]] as a new section in "Forms", after
    <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#the-fieldset-element">the
    <code>fieldset</code> element</a>.
1. Add <{collector}> to the lists of [=flow content=], [=phrasing content=], [=palpable content=],
    [=form-associated elements=], and [=listed=], [=labelable=], and [=submittable=] elements.
1. In the definition of a [=form control/disabled=] form control, add: "or it is a descendant of a
    <{collector}> element whose <{collector/disabled}> attribute is specified".
1. Apply the changes in [[#patch-static-validation]] and [[#patch-construct-the-entry-list]].
1. In [=interactively validate the constraints=], add the requirements in [[#reporting]].
1. In "Pseudo-classes", add <{collector}> elements to the elements that <code>:enabled</code>,
    <code>:disabled</code>, <code>:required</code>, <code>:optional</code>, <code>:valid</code>,
    <code>:invalid</code>, <code>:user-valid</code>, and <code>:user-invalid</code> can match.
1. In <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/forms.html#the-label-element">the
    <code>label</code> element</a>, add: "If a <{label}> element's [=labeled control=] is a
    <{collector}> element, the <{label}> element's activation behavior should run the focusing steps
    for the first [=focusable=] element among the collector's [=enabled collected controls=]."
1. In the indices, add the <{collector}> element, its attributes, and the {{HTMLCollectorElement}}
    interface.


<h2 id=a11y>Accessibility considerations</h2>

* A <{collector}> element maps to the <code>group</code> role [[WAI-ARIA-1.2]]. Its accessible name
    comes from its <{label}> elements, or from <code>aria-label</code> and
    <code>aria-labelledby</code>.
* User agents should expose the collector as required when it has a <{collector/required}>
    attribute, and as invalid when it matches <code>:user-invalid</code>.
* Authors should still give each collected control its own accessible name, such as "Area code",
    because assistive technology announces the pieces one at a time as the user moves through them.
* Showing one message for the whole value, and moving focus to the piece that needs attention, gives
    screen reader users one clear error instead of a separate announcement for every piece.

Issue: Define how <{collector}> maps to platform accessibility APIs in [[HTML-AAM-1.0]], including
how the collector's error message is exposed on its collected controls.


<h2 id=security-privacy>Security and privacy considerations</h2>

* This specification exposes no new information. A collector's value contains only the values of
    controls in the same document, which script can already read.
* A collector only collects controls in its own [=tree=] with the same [=form owner=]. It can't read
    controls in another document, such as a cross-origin <code>iframe</code>, or inside a shadow
    tree.
* The <{collector/pattern}> attribute is plain text substitution. It can't run script, fetch
    resources, or evaluate expressions.
* A collected control in the Password state still has its value combined into the collector's
    value in the clear, as script could already combine it. Authors should avoid putting passwords
    in a collector.


<h2 id=more-examples>More examples</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

<div class=example id=example-country-code>
    A <{select}> element can be a piece of the value. If the user types a number but hasn't chosen a
    country yet, the collector is suffering from a pattern mismatch.

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <label for="mobile">Mobile number</label>
    <collector id="mobile" name="mobile" pattern="+{country} {number}">
      <select name="country" autocomplete="tel-country-code" aria-label="Country code">
        <option value="">Country</option>
        <option value="1">United States (+1)</option>
        <option value="44">United Kingdom (+44)</option>
      </select>
      <input type="tel" name="number" autocomplete="tel-national" aria-label="Number">
    </collector>
    </xmp>

    Choosing United Kingdom and typing <kbd>7700 900123</kbd> submits
    <code>mobile=+44 7700 900123</code>. Leaving both blank submits <code>mobile=</code>.
</div>

<div class=example id=example-one-time-code>
    Without a <{collector/pattern}> attribute, a collector joins all its collectable controls in tree
    order. The pieces don't need names.

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <form action="/verify" method="post">
      <label for="code">Enter the 6-digit code we sent you</label>
      <collector id="code" name="code" required>
        <input maxlength="1" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="one-time-code" aria-label="Digit 1">
        <input maxlength="1" inputmode="numeric" aria-label="Digit 2">
        <input maxlength="1" inputmode="numeric" aria-label="Digit 3">
        <input maxlength="1" inputmode="numeric" aria-label="Digit 4">
        <input maxlength="1" inputmode="numeric" aria-label="Digit 5">
        <input maxlength="1" inputmode="numeric" aria-label="Digit 6">
      </collector>
      <button>Verify</button>
    </form>
    </xmp>

    The form submits <code>code=482913</code>. It can't be submitted with only some of the digits.
</div>

<div class=example id=example-custom-message>
    Authors can replace the user agent's message with their own, and check the whole value with
    script.

    <xmp highlight=js>
    const expiry = document.querySelector("collector[name=cc-exp]"); // pattern="{month}/{year}"

    expiry.addEventListener("change", () => {
      const [month, year] = expiry.value.split("/").map(Number);
      const now = new Date();
      const expired = year + 2000 < now.getFullYear() ||
                      (year + 2000 === now.getFullYear() && month < now.getMonth() + 1);
      expiry.setCustomValidity(expired ? "This card has expired." : "");
    });
    </xmp>
</div>


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

<h3 id=alt-linked-form-controls>Linked form controls</h3>

<a href="https://arronei.github.io/HTML-Additions/linked-form-controls/spec/">Linked Form
Controls</a> takes a different approach to the same family of problems. It adds a <code>link</code>
attribute to <{input}> and <{select}> elements that names the next control in a chain. Controls that
are linked and share a <{input/name}> are submitted as one entry, with their values concatenated.

The two designs differ in these ways:

<table class=data>
    <thead>
        <tr><th><th><{collector}> element<th><code>link</code> attribute
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>How pieces are grouped<td>By containment in one element<td>By ID references from each control to the next
        <tr><td>Separators and ordering<td>Any, through <{collector/pattern}><td>None; values are joined in link order
        <tr><td>Whole-value validation<td>Yes, with a rollup error message<td>No; each control is validated on its own
        <tr><td>Focus moves as pieces fill<td>No<td>Yes, along with pasting and autofill spread across the pieces
        <tr><td>Without support<td>Each piece submitted under its own name<td>Each piece submitted under the shared name
</table>

The designs don't conflict. Collected controls could also be linked, so that a collector provides the
value and validation and the links provide the editing behavior.


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

Issue: <strong>Element name.</strong> The name <code>collector</code> needs checking against existing
content that uses it as an unknown element. Alternatives include <code>compound</code>,
<code>fieldgroup</code>, and <code>composite</code>.

Issue: <strong>Identifiers.</strong> Placeholders match the <{input/name}> attribute, so that a
collector can be copied without ID clashes and so that user agents without support submit
meaningful entries. Should they match <code>id</code> instead, or as well?

Issue: <strong>Setting the value.</strong> Should {{HTMLCollectorElement/value}} be settable, or
should there be a <code>value</code> content attribute, that splits a whole value into the pieces by
matching it against the pattern? That would also let the user agent autofill a whole value, such as
an <code>autocomplete="tel"</code> number, into the pieces. Adjacent placeholders such as
<code>{a}{b}</code> can't be split without extra information, such as each piece's
<code>maxlength</code>.

Issue: <strong>Moving focus.</strong> Should the user agent move focus to the next collected control
when one fills up, and spread pasted text across the pieces?

Issue: <strong>More controls.</strong> Should <{textarea}> elements, checkboxes, radio buttons, and
form-associated custom elements be collectable? Should a collector be allowed inside another and act
as one of its pieces?

Issue: <strong>Validity flag.</strong> A partly filled collector reports
{{ValidityState/patternMismatch}}. Would a new flag, such as <code>incomplete</code>, be clearer?

Issue: <strong>Unmatched placeholders.</strong> A placeholder that matches no control is silently
replaced with nothing. Should the collector be invalid instead, so that authoring mistakes show up in
testing?

Issue: <strong>Partial submission.</strong> When validation is skipped, should a partly filled
collector submit its partial value, as specified, or the empty string?
